<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Franz Ferdinand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archduke Franz Ferdinand was the nephew of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> and heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne from 1896. He favoured army reform and greater rights for the empire's Slav peoples, and his morganatic marriage to Sophie Chotek strained relations at court. His assassination with his wife in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a> set off the crisis that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria.jpg" alt="Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Schmutzer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Schmutzer_-_Franz_Ferdinand_von_%C3%96sterreich-Este,_um_1914_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
